rms:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101 - 1001) + (989899)(1001001 - 989899).\n\\]\nSimplifying the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n1010101 - 1001 = 1000100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1001001 - 989899 = 11102.\n\\]\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(11102).\n\\]\nNow, we need to find the rightmost non-zero digit of this expression. We will do this by considering the last digits of each term and their product.\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cc49bf70bbb55964e1b44778e42aadb39ea37d24e6b9de4d04b907792e303ee2"},"seconds":41.07772660255432,"seed":173468078898446229,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit in the expression \\((1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001)\\), we will simplify and analyze the expression step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001).\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out common terms:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101 - 1001) + (989899)(1001001 - 989899).\n\\]\nSimplifying the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n1010101 - 1001 = 1000100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1001001 - 989899 = 11102.\n\\]\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(11102).\n\\]\n\nTo determine the rightmost non-zero digit, we need to look at the last digits of the numbers involved:\n- The last digit of \\(1001001\\) is 1.\n- The last digit of \\(1000100\\) is 0.\n- The last digit of \\(989899\\) is 9.\n- The last digit of \\(11102\\) is 2.\n\nNow, we can analyze the last digits of the products:\n- The last digit of \\((1001001)(1000100)\\) is the last digit of \\(1 \\cdot 0 = 0\\).\n- The last digit of \\((989899)(11102)\\) is the last digit of \\(9 \\cdot 2 = 18\\), which is 8.\n\nThus, the last digit of the entire expression is the last digit of \\(0 + 8 = 8\\). Since there are no trailing zeros (other than those from the term \\((1001001)(1000100)\\), which are canceled out by the non-zero digit in the second term), the rightmost non-zero digit is 8, and it is followed by 0 zeroes.\n\nTherefore, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(8, 0)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(8, 0)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit in the expression \\((1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001)\\), we will simplify and analyze the expression step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001).\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out common terms:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101 - 1001) + (989899)(1001001 - 989899).\n\\]\nSimplifying the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n1010101 - 1001 = 1000100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1001001 - 989899 = 11102.\n\\]\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(11102).\n\\]\n\nTo determine the rightmost non-zero digit, we need to look at the last digits of the numbers involved:\n- The last digit of \\(1001001\\) is 1.\n- The last digit of \\(1000100\\) is 0.\n- The last digit of \\(989899\\) is 9.\n- The last digit of \\(11102\\) is 2.\n\nNow, we can analyze the last digits of the products:\n- The last digit of \\((1001001)(1000100)\\) is the last digit of \\(1 \\cdot 0 = 0\\).\n- The last digit of \\((989899)(11102)\\) is the last digit of \\(9 \\cdot 2 = 18\\), which is 8.\n\nThus, the last digit of the entire expression is the last digit of \\(0 + 8 = 8\\). Since there are no trailing zeros (other than those from the term \\((1001001)(1000100)\\), which are canceled out by the non-zero digit in the second term), the rightmost non-zero digit is 8, and it is followed by 0 zeroes.\n\nTherefore, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(8, 0)}\\).","id":"algebra:117"}
